WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
Hey Thoughty2 here.
 
As far back as I can remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.480
I always wanted to tell you about gangsters.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.740
Like it or not, mafia mythology is murderously&nbsp;
melded with the history of modern America. To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.600
some, the Mob represents an aspirational way&nbsp;
of life full of girls, guns, and glamour.
 &nbsp;

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:26.940
But there is of course a much darker side. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:31.440
is after all an organisation that relies&nbsp;
on fear to get what it wants and is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:36.960
likely to resolve disputes with a bullet&nbsp;
behind the ear than a quiet word in it.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:43.380
Whatever your take on la Cosa Nostra, they’ve&nbsp;
played an undeniable role in shaping our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:49.920
culture. After all, which of us doesn’t have&nbsp;
a favourite Mob movie and who can’t mis-quote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.380
a piece of iconic dialogue on command (see&nbsp;
the opening of this video for an example.)

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.180
But where did it all begin,&nbsp;
this obsession of ours? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.320
when it comes to the extraordinary&nbsp;
rise, and fall, of the Mafia in America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.280
it can all be traced back to&nbsp;
the so-called Five Families.
 &nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:14.520
These powerful clans ran New York City for&nbsp;
decades making money from legitimate businesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:20.640
while also profiting from illegal gambling,&nbsp;
protection rackets and contract murder. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.680
In doing so they established the blueprint&nbsp;
which would see organised crime spread&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:30.480
across America and perhaps even have&nbsp;
a hand in the death of a President. 
 &nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:35.640
Trust me, if you think your family is bad,&nbsp;
wait until you get a load of this lot.
 &nbsp;

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:43.200
The origins of the Mafia can be traced back to&nbsp;
the 18th century and the Italian island of Sicily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:51.120
if Italy is a boot, Sicily is it’s amputated toe.
Over the years Sicily has been ruled by many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.820
different foreign invaders - everyone from&nbsp;
the Romans to the French - but eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.460
boot or no boot, the people grew sick and tired of&nbsp;
being kicked about and so they banded together. 
 &nbsp;

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:09.180
The groups they formed slowly evolved into&nbsp;
private armies, who in time grew so powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.980
they could extort money from the very landowners&nbsp;
who were supposed to be controlling them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:20.100
These armies became known as families,&nbsp;
each of which had its own secret codes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.460
and ways of meting out rough justice. 
 
In the late 19th and early 20th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:31.980
a great many Italians emigrated to the United&nbsp;
States in search of a better life. This included a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:38.040
large number from Sicily, where poor economic and&nbsp;
social conditions made the treacherous trip across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:43.560
the Atlantic seem like an attractive option.
Many of these new arrivals settled in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.900
particularly in a spot on the Lower East side&nbsp;
of Manhattan that came to be known as Little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:54.060
Italy (for obvious reasons). Of course, it&nbsp;
wasn’t long before criminals from the old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:58.920
country began to exploit this burgeoning&nbsp;
immigrant population across the pond.
 &nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:03.960
The notorious Five Points Gang, founded&nbsp;
by former boxer Paolo Vaccarelli,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:11.340
emerged in 1890 and terrorized New York for thirty&nbsp;
years. Meanwhile spawning gangsters who would go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:17.340
on to be some of the most feared crime bosses&nbsp;
ever, including Meyer Lansky, Bugsy Siegel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:23.220
even old Scarface himself, Al Capone. 
 
In 1920 Prohibition hit America. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:29.160
surprisingly, telling people they could no longer&nbsp;
drink alcohol went down about as well as turning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.820
up to a high school fancy dress party as Jimmy&nbsp;
Saville. The U.S. government might have believed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:42.060
it was a brilliant idea, but the general public&nbsp;
most certainly did not, and it was boom time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:48.180
for anyone who could supply bootleg booze. 
 
This was the iconic era of trench coats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:54.120
Tommy guns, as upstanding crime fighters&nbsp;
like Elliot Ness battled the Mob on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.920
mean streets of New York and Chicago. 
 
Prohibition in the U.S. coincided with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:05.520
the rise of fascism in Italy as Benito Mussolini&nbsp;
came to power. A tolerant attitude towards crime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.720
or indeed anything really, is&nbsp;
not in the brief of a dictator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:15.780
and Mussolini came down hard on the Mafia, causing&nbsp;
a large number of its members to head to America.
 &nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.080
This perfect storm of a major business&nbsp;
opportunity and lots of new recruits saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:28.140
the American Mafia grow in size and strength&nbsp;
throughout the 1920s. For the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.140 --> 00:04:33.000
a man by the name of Joe ‘The Boss’&nbsp;
Masseria was running things in those days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:41.280
but a powerful Sicilian Mafia Don wanted to take&nbsp;
over, so he sent his protégé, Salvatore Maranzano,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.160
to make a big bid for power in the Big Apple.
 
Maranzano gradually built up his own criminal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.900
empire before declaring all-out&nbsp;
war on Masseria in 1930.
 &nbsp;

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.460
For over a year the two New York&nbsp;
Mafia clans openly fought one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.460 --> 00:05:01.260
with gang members on both sides gunned down&nbsp;
in the streets on a number of occasions. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:01.260 --> 00:05:10.020
Eventually, on April 15th, 1931, Masseria himself&nbsp;
was killed in a restaurant by four gunmen. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:18.240
person who’d arranged this mealtime murder was&nbsp;
his own right-hand-man, Charles ‘Lucky’ Luciano.
 &nbsp;

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:24.540
Lucky was a gangster to his very core; as a&nbsp;
teenager he cut his teeth in the Five Points Gang,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:29.220
before making his fortune in bootlegging,&nbsp;
and got his nickname after surviving a knife&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:32.820
attack that left him with a drooping eye&nbsp;
and distinctive scars across his face. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:38.940
When Luciano realised the war was going badly&nbsp;
he switched sides, betraying his boss on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:45.060
condition that, if Maranzano emerged victorious,&nbsp;
Luciano himself would be made second-in-command.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:50.820
Upon winning, Marazano promptly re-organised the&nbsp;
Mafia gangs of New York into the so-called Five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:57.780
Families. In a bid to consolidate his new-found&nbsp;
power, he then named himself ‘Capo dei capi,’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:03.840
‘the boss of all the bosses.’ But the snazzy&nbsp;
title didn’t go down all that well with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:09.780
heads of the Five Families. They already disliked&nbsp;
Maranzano, who they saw as greedy, arrogant and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:15.480
old fashioned, and the attempted power-grab proved&nbsp;
to be the literal last nail in his coffin. 
 &nbsp;

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:21.660
Four hitmen posing as government agents went&nbsp;
to Maranzano’s office, disarmed his bodyguards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:27.900
then made sure that he too would be sleeping&nbsp;
with the fishes. Once again it was ‘Lucky’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:32.400
Luciano who set up the hit; you know&nbsp;
I’m beginning to think that he can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:37.980
be trusted at all. What’s the world coming to&nbsp;
when a Mafia boss can’t be taken at his word?
 &nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.760
Anyway, with both of the old-school bosses&nbsp;
now out of the way, it was time for the Mafia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:50.940
modernisers to have their turn. Given that he’d&nbsp;
so ably got rid of the two previous big dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:57.540
it would have been reasonable to assume ‘Lucky’&nbsp;
Luciano would take the throne and declare himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:01.920
the new Boss of Bosses.
 
But he didn’t. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.740
In fairness, having personally ordered the&nbsp;
murders of the previous two incumbents he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:13.680
knew all too well how dangerous it was to&nbsp;
be the Mafia’s head honcho. So instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.960
he established something called The Commission,&nbsp;
which was made up of seven Mafia leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.700
including the heads of all of the Five Families,&nbsp;
and would be responsible for overseeing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:30.480
activities of the Mob and mediating disputes&nbsp;
between rival gangs. They met every five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.480 --> 00:07:36.720
to iron out major problems and just generally&nbsp;
put the organisation into organised crime. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:43.560
Now, if there’s one you don’t expect from the&nbsp;
Mafia it’s high-level admin. Putting horse’s heads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:50.340
in people’s beds, sure, we’re all onboard with&nbsp;
that, but a HR department, that’s just weird..
 &nbsp;

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:56.400
With a solid structure in place and war&nbsp;
behind them for now, the Five Families&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:02.640
started to move to a whole ‘nother level. But&nbsp;
as alcohol was decriminalised once again, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.560
needed to look for new avenues to make money.
 
The traditional money spinners like robbery,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:11.100
extortion, loan sharking, and money&nbsp;
laundering all continued as normal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:18.420
but it was time to diversify the family business.
 
So, they infiltrated the labour unions, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:24.360
allowed them to establish themselves in industries&nbsp;
like construction, trucking and, the career of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:30.960
choice of Tony Soprano, waste management. 
 
In the 1940s, the city of Las Vegas was starting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:37.200
its meteoric rise to become the greatest centre&nbsp;
of vice on God’s green earth. Despite being on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:42.360
opposite coast to the Five Families’ traditional&nbsp;
stomping grounds, the promise of profits proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.360 --> 00:08:48.180
too pretty to pass up. They bankrolled a&nbsp;
number of casinos as Sin City swelled and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:54.480
would skim money from the huge sums of cash taken&nbsp;
on the floors every night, a scam thought to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.480 --> 00:09:01.080
netted them hundreds of millions of dollars.
 
For decades it seemed as if the Mafia was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.880
untouchable. Their code of silence,&nbsp;
known as omerta, meant that members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:10.380
were sworn to secrecy and forbidden to&nbsp;
cooperate with police under fear of death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:16.020
Infiltrating the Families was practically&nbsp;
impossible, because allegiances often went back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.020 --> 00:09:22.620
generations to the same small towns in Italy.
 
The US government seemed woefully behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:29.220
the curve as the Mob continued to grow in&nbsp;
both power and numbers. But then, slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:35.220
law enforcement started to fight back. 
 
In 1950 a special committee was set up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:40.320
investigate the existence of organised crime&nbsp;
and it held hearings in fourteen cities across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.280
the country, including testimonies&nbsp;
from over six hundred witnesses. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:48.720
The hearings were televised at a time when&nbsp;
TV was just starting to become massive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:54.540
and it was the first time most Americans&nbsp;
had even heard anything about this shadowy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:59.400
organisation which seemed to exert influence&nbsp;
over huge swathes of their country. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:09.180
All of this attention was&nbsp;
obviously bad news for the Mob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:14.040
but if the ‘50s started poorly for the Mafia,&nbsp;
it was nothing compared to how they ended. 
 &nbsp;

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:19.620
In 1957, a meeting was arranged in the&nbsp;
small town of Apalachin in upstate New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:23.640
York. It took place at the house&nbsp;
of a man known as Joe The Barber.

00:10:23.640 --> 00:10:30.540
This coming together of the clans was a national&nbsp;
Mafia meeting with more than one hundred important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:37.620
crime bosses and assorted henchmen in attendance&nbsp;
to discuss all kinds of nefarious activities and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.620 --> 00:10:41.460
one can only assume, have a bloody&nbsp;
good catch-up while they were at it.

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.800
The problem was that Apalachin was little&nbsp;
more than a hamlet, the sort of place where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.120
big news would usually be the mom and pop&nbsp;
store on Main Street running out of milk.&nbsp;

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:56.460
When numerous flash cars arrived and deposited&nbsp;
a large number of extremely well-dressed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:59.280
severe looking gentlemen in the middle of town,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:03.780
the local police force couldn’t help but&nbsp;
wonder just what the hell was going on.

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:09.960
They raided the property, causing the highly&nbsp;
unusual, and not a little humiliating scene of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:15.120
dozens of gangsters running away to&nbsp;
hide in the nearby woods. Amazingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:21.900
this shrub-based counterintelligence tactic&nbsp;
didn’t work and sixty of them were arrested.

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:27.780
Not only was this a very embarrassing incident&nbsp;
for the Mafia, but it once again shed light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:33.360
on what was supposed to be a top-secret&nbsp;
organisation. Although any sense of secrecy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:41.040
would completely disappear just two years later.
 
In 1959 a mobster called Joe Valachi was convicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:47.460
of drug trafficking and sentenced to fifteen&nbsp;
years in prison. While inside he murdered a fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:53.700
inmate, a move that saw his sentence extended to&nbsp;
life behind bars with no chance of parole. Clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:58.500
the prospect of eating slop from a tray and trying&nbsp;
really bloody hard not to drop his soap in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:05.940
shower didn’t thrill Valachi, because in 1963&nbsp;
he agreed to testify about his time in the Mob.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:13.860
This was the first time a Made Man had ever taken&nbsp;
the stand and publicly admitted the Mafia existed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:20.040
let alone gone into details about its structure&nbsp;
and activities. Valachi even went so far as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.040 --> 00:12:25.140
name many current and former gangsters,&nbsp;
including bosses of the major families.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:29.820
Valachi’s hearing was part of a crusade&nbsp;
by Attorney General Robert Kennedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:35.100
who’d made it his personal mission to target&nbsp;
organised crime. He did a brilliant job of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.100 --> 00:12:40.260
too, with mobster convictions rising eight&nbsp;
hundred percent during his time in office.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.220
That’s one of the reasons some&nbsp;
people believe the Mafia played a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:50.820
major role in the assassination of his brother,&nbsp;
President John F. Kennedy, in November 1963.
 &nbsp;

00:12:50.820 --> 00:12:55.980
Seismic though the killing of President&nbsp;
Kennedy was, it couldn’t stop the events&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.980 --> 00:13:01.200
that Valachi’s confession had set in motion. 
 
Several laws were subsequently passed by Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:06.480
to give the FBI greater powers to tackle the&nbsp;
Mafia head-on, the most ground-breaking being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.720
the so-called RICO Act of 1970. 
 
The Act essentially made it illegal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:18.720
to be a part of a quote ‘ongoing criminal&nbsp;
enterprise,’ regardless of whether or not you were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:24.600
actually caught committing any crimes. That made&nbsp;
it significantly easier to convict known mobsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:30.660
but more importantly it suddenly made it possible&nbsp;
to go after mob bosses who were unlikely to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:35.880
get directly involved in the dirty work their&nbsp;
organisations were built on. The thinking was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:41.160
that this would mean more convictions and&nbsp;
longer sentences, and boy did it work.
 &nbsp;

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.860
The man at the spearhead of this&nbsp;
revolution was US Attorney Rudy Giuliani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.860 --> 00:13:54.300
and he wasted no time wreaking RICO havoc. In&nbsp;
1985 he brought the heads of three of the Five&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.300 --> 00:14:00.000
Families to trial using the RICO statute&nbsp;
in what was dubbed the ‘case of cases.’ 
 &nbsp;

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:05.340
And it turned out to be a case&nbsp;
of Giuliani 1 – Five Families 0,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.340 --> 00:14:09.840
as all three mob bosses were sentenced&nbsp;
to one hundred years in prison.

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:16.500
It set a precedent for the next thirty years, with&nbsp;
Mafia bosses frequently given major jail time when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:22.260
brought to court as part of a RICO investigation.&nbsp;
And as surveillance became more sophisticated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:27.420
the FBI also increasingly used&nbsp;
wiretaps to ensnare mobsters.
 &nbsp;

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:33.000
Faced with huge sentences and often&nbsp;
having incriminated themselves on tape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:38.760
more and more gangsters conveniently&nbsp;
forgot about their oath of silence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:43.080
happily sang like canaries in order&nbsp;
to spend less time behind bars. 
 &nbsp;

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:49.500
Even those in the Mafia who seemed&nbsp;
untouchable were eventually, well, touched.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:55.380
John Gotti was nicknamed the Teflon Don because&nbsp;
nothing ever seemed to stick to him; a flashy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.380 --> 00:15:00.660
dresser who actively courted publicity, when he&nbsp;
took over as head of the Gambino family in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.660 --> 00:15:06.660
mid-80s it was the richest of the Five Families,&nbsp;
with an annual income of half a billion dollars. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:11.760
But after years as the most flamboyant&nbsp;
and famous gangster in New York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:19.020
his best friend and underboss Sammy ‘The&nbsp;
Bull’ Gravano testified against him and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:26.220
1992 Gotti was sent to prison for life with no&nbsp;
possibility of parole. He died in jail in 2002.
 &nbsp;

00:15:26.220 --> 00:15:30.420
With the balance of power having now tipped&nbsp;
firmly in favour of the federal government,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:37.380
the influence of the Mafia continued to dwindle,&nbsp;
and the decline was only compounded by increased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:43.440
competition from other gangs, including the&nbsp;
Russian Mafia. Same name, furrier hats. 
 &nbsp;

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:49.440
But that doesn’t mean that America is now Cosa&nbsp;
Nostra free. In fact, far from it. The attacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:55.320
on 9/11 saw a huge switch in the FBI’s&nbsp;
focus from organized crime to terrorism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.320 --> 00:16:03.060
with some Mafia task forces dropping from&nbsp;
around four hundred agents to as few as twenty.
 &nbsp;

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:12.480
As recently as 2019 the FBI estimated there&nbsp;
were still as many as three thousand active&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:20.520
members of the Mafia in the US, and the legendary&nbsp;
Five Families continue to operate to this day. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:25.380
while it’s fair to say that the glory days&nbsp;
of the American Mafia are far behind them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:31.500
when it comes to the Mob only a fool would&nbsp;
say that you should ‘fugget-about-it.’
 &nbsp;

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:33.240
Thanks for watching.

